: Let's say we have a hash table with N elements. When we insert a key, we compute its hash value, which gives us an index into the underlying array. If that index already contains a value (i.e., a collision), we need to handle it. Common methods include chaining (each bucket is a linked list) or open addressing (searching for an empty slot). For lookups, the average time complexity is O(1), assuming a good hash function and load factor. In the worst case, if many elements hash to the same index, it could degrade to O(n). So, the hash table provides fast average-case operations, but collisions need to be managed.
</think>
